This study represents the first investigation of size and shape variations in the palatine bones and nasal openings (choanae) of the extinct bear species Ursus kanivetz and Ursus rossicus, compared with modern bear species. Measurements of choanal height and width were taken from CT scans of cave bear and modern bear skulls. Statistical analysis confirmed no significant size differences in nasal openings between male and female cave bears. Researchers calculated comparative size indices for both palatine bones and choanae across all examined species. The study established, for the first time in bears, a strong correlation between palatine bone shape and average winter temperatures in their habitats. However, the relationship between nasal opening size and winter temperatures proved weaker and statistically insignificant. These findings provide additional information about the respiratory system characteristics of cave bears.
